/* book Worked example 2: Q: The network with a controlled source in Thevenin's and Norton's theorems (worked example 2) has Vth = 9 V and Rth = 3 Ω. Find the maximum power any load can take, and compare it with the power in a 6 Ω load.
   1. Rth must come from the test-source or Voc/Isc method because of the controlled source: Rth = 3 Ω.
   2. Maximum power load: RL = 3 Ω.
   3. Pmax = 9²/(4 × 3) = 81/12 = 6.75 W. Check: I = 9/6 = 1.5 A, 1.5² × 3 = 6.75 W.
   4. 6 Ω load: I = 9/(3 + 6) = 1 A, P = 1² × 6 = 6 W, about 89 % of the maximum.
   5. Had the controlled source been wrongly switched off (Rth = 4 Ω), the predicted best load would be 4 Ω; the true power in 4 Ω is 81 × 4/49 = 6.61 W, short of 6.75 W.
   Answer: RL = 3 Ω takes 6.75 W; a 6 Ω load takes 6 W. */
