/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Statement.
   - Bilateral means each element behaves the same for either direction of current: R, L and C are bilateral; diodes and transistors are not.
   - Why it holds: with R, L and C only, the nodal matrix G is symmetric (Gkj = Gjk), and the transfer from node j to node k is the same as from k to j.
   - Take n branches in parallel between nodes A and B; branch k is a voltage source Vk (+ towards A) in series with Rk, or a plain resistor (Vk = 0).
   - VAB = Σ(Vk/Rk) / Σ(1/Rk)
   - VAB is the voltage of A above B (volts); Vk is the source in branch k, entered with a minus sign if its + faces B; Rk is the branch resistance (ohms).
   - With conductances, VAB = ΣVkGk/ΣGk.
   - Statement.
   - The proof uses only KCL, KVL and the shape of the network (its graph), never what the elements are.
   - As a check: in worked example 1 of mesh analysis the branch powers are −144 W (source), 72, 36, 18 and 18 W, which add to zero.
*/
